          <figcaption class="ar-cap">From the supplied production environment: printing on the T-shirt production line</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>This guide is for buyers who have already concluded they need a factory rather than a print-on-demand shop. You get an evaluation framework, the technical questions that separate real manufacturers from trading intermediaries, and selection criteria that shift with your order profile. Where numbers matter, we give working ranges and tell you what to lock in writing before the deposit leaves your account. YAUUM Sourcing Team writes from the production side of private-label men's apparel, building T-shirts, hoodies, jackets, pants, sportswear, and streetwear for brands, wholesalers, and e-commerce sellers</p>

        <h2 id="why-it-matters">Why Choosing the Right Custom T-Shirt Manufacturer Decides Your Brand's Future</h2>

        <p>A T-shirt is the cheapest garment to make and the easiest to get wrong. Thin fabric, tight margins, and a customer who notices a collar that twists after three washes or a body length 2cm short of the spec you approved. Two supplier types dominate search results, and they solve different problems</p>

        <div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table"><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Retail / Print-on-Demand Platform</th><th>OEM / ODM Factory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Order size</td><td>1–100 pieces typical</td><td>MOQ-based, commonly 300–500 pcs/color for cut-and-sew</td></tr><tr><td>Unit cost at scale</td><td>$12–28/pc retail-equivalent</td><td>FOB roughly $2.8–6.5/pc (180gsm combed cotton crew neck, 3,000 pcs)</td></tr><tr><td>Fabric control</td><td>Catalog blanks only</td><td>Custom GSM, blend, yarn count, and finish</td></tr><tr><td>Labeling</td><td>Limited or none</td><td>Woven label, neck label, size label, care label, hang tag</td></tr><tr><td>Lead time</td><td>3–10 days</td><td>Sampling 7–15 days, bulk 30–45 days, sea freight 25–35 days</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

        <p>"Custom" means two different things here. On a platform, it means your artwork on someone else's blank. With a factory, it means the garment itself: 180gsm versus 240gsm, shoulder seam placement, neck rib width at 2cm or 2.5cm, enzyme wash or raw finish. Only the second route builds a product your competitor cannot order from the same catalog page next Tuesday</p>

        <h2 id="evaluation-framework">The 8-Point Evaluation Framework for Vetting a T-Shirt Factory</h2>

        <p>Run every candidate through these eight dimensions and score each one. Any dimension where you cannot get a straight answer counts as a red flag, not a neutral</p>

        <p><strong>1. Fabric capability.</strong> Can they specify fabric by composition, GSM, yarn count, and finish, and send the mill spec sheet? A supplier who answers "cotton, good quality" is a trading company or a ten-machine workshop</p>

        <p><strong>2. Decoration methods.</strong> Screen printing, DTG, heat transfer, and embroidery: which run in-house, and which get subcontracted? In-house breadth gives you a single point of accountability when a print placement drifts 10mm</p>

        <p><strong>3. Sampling process.</strong> Is the sample sewn on the same line as bulk, by the same operators? Is the sample fee credited against bulk? How many revision rounds are included?</p>

        <p><strong>4. MOQ logic.</strong> Ask what drives the minimum: mill fabric quantity, dyehouse lot size, or screen and digitizing setup. The driver tells you where negotiation room exists</p>

        <p><strong>5. Quality control.</strong> What happens at in-line inspection versus final inspection, and do they work to a stated AQL level?</p>

        <p><strong>6. Compliance documentation.</strong> Fabric composition, color fastness, dimensional stability, restricted substances: which reports can they hand over, and which need a third-party lab? Ask now, not when customs holds your container</p>

        <p><strong>7. Communication efficiency.</strong> Response time in hours, ability to read a tech pack without hand-holding, and whether they raise problems during production or after the goods ship</p>

        <p><strong>8. Reorder consistency.</strong> How are approved samples, lab dips, and print screens stored, and for how long? The answer predicts whether order two matches order one</p>

        <p>For reference on what a full in-house scope looks like: YAUUM runs screen print, DTG, heat transfer, and embroidery under one roof, with cut-and-sew tee programs starting at 300 pieces per color</p>

        <p>Want to score candidates side by side? Talk to our team with your shortlist</p>

        <h2 id="fabric-gsm">Fabric &amp; GSM: What Your Manufacturer Should Be Able to Tell You</h2>

        <p>Most sourcing arguments start with fabric, because buyer and factory often mean different things by the same word</p>

        <p><strong>100% cotton</strong> delivers the best hand feel and the cleanest print surface, shrinks more, and costs more. <strong>CVC (chief value cotton, typically 60/40 cotton-poly)</strong> trades a little softness for dimensional stability and carries most retail core programs. <strong>TC (poly-cotton, usually 65/35)</strong> is durable and cheap, with a drier hand that buyers either accept or reject immediately. <strong>100% polyester</strong> belongs in performance and sublimation work</p>

        <p>GSM, grams per square meter, is the weight dial:</p>

        <div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table"><thead><tr><th>Fabric</th><th>Typical GSM</th><th>Best For</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lightweight cotton jersey</td><td>140–160</td><td>Summer basics, layering, warm climates</td></tr><tr><td>Standard retail jersey</td><td>170–190</td><td>Year-round core tees, screen print</td></tr><tr><td>Heavyweight jersey</td><td>200–240</td><td>Streetwear, oversized fits, high-end positioning</td></tr><tr><td>French terry (for sweatshirts)</td><td>280–400</td><td>Hoodies and crewnecks, not tees</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

        <p>Write a tolerance into the spec. A ±5% GSM band is normal mill practice, and stating it keeps a 180gsm order from arriving at 165gsm with everyone shrugging</p>

        <p>Pantone matching deserves direct pressure during supplier calls. The same Pantone number reads differently on 100% cotton, CVC, and polyester, because dye uptake varies by fiber. A factory that promises an exact match without dipping your actual fabric is either inexperienced or selling. Require a lab dip on production fabric, approve it in writing, then release bulk dyeing</p>

        <p>On shrinkage and color fastness, ask which test method the factory references and what acceptance criteria it works to. Common targets sit under 5% shrinkage after three home launderings and grade 4 or better on the grey scale, with the exact standard set by your destination market. Our <a href="/t-shirt-manufacturer/">private label t-shirt manufacturing</a> workflow builds these numbers into the spec sheet before sampling starts</p>

        <h2 id="decoration-methods">Printing &amp; Decoration Methods: Screen Print vs DTG vs Heat Transfer vs Embroidery</h2>

        <p>Decoration is a volume-and-durability calculation. Choose wrong and you either overpay per unit or ship a garment that fails in the third wash</p>

        <div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table"><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Best Order Size</th><th>Color Range</th><th>Durability</th><th>Relative Unit Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Screen print</td><td>100–50,000+ pcs</td><td>1–8 spot colors, plastisol or water-based</td><td>50+ home washes with minimal fade</td><td>$0.40–1.80/pc at 500–3,000 pcs</td></tr><tr><td>DTG (direct to garment)</td><td>1–200 pcs</td><td>Full color, no screen setup</td><td>Holds 25–40 washes, softer hand</td><td>$3.00–8.00/pc</td></tr><tr><td>Heat transfer</td><td>50–2,000 pcs</td><td>Full color, fine gradients</td><td>Cracks at fold lines under heavy wear</td><td>$1.20–3.50/pc</td></tr><tr><td>Embroidery</td><td>50–5,000+ pcs</td><td>Up to 12 thread colors</td><td>Outlasts the garment</td><td>$1.50–4.50/pc, stitch-count driven</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

        <p>Decoration cost moves with artwork size, stitch count, and the number of placements, so a two-placement design never prices like a left-chest logo</p>

        <p>The decision rule is short. Below roughly 100 pieces per design, DTG wins because nobody pays for screens. Above 100 pieces, screen setup amortizes and unit price falls hard; by 500 pieces it is usually the cheapest durable option on the table. Heat transfer covers full-color art at moderate volume where screen counts would get absurd. Embroidery is the default for left-chest logos and high-end positioning, and it pairs cleanly with a screen-printed back graphic</p>

        <p>Watch for one capability signal. A factory running all four processes in-house can recommend honestly, because no single method pays its rent. If a supplier pushes DTG for a 5,000-piece order, ask what equipment they actually own. Our <a href="/our-services/">custom t-shirt printing services</a> page shows how multi-method work gets scheduled across a single production window</p>

        <h2 id="moq-sampling-pricing">MOQ, Sampling &amp; Pricing Logic: How to Read a Factory Quote</h2>

        <p>MOQ is not a policy. It is the sum of three cost floors: mill fabric minimum, dyehouse lot minimum, and decoration setup covering screens, digitizing, and plates</p>

        <p>That arithmetic explains why MOQ is quoted per color. One color in one fabric might start at 300 pieces. Add a second colorway and the second color carries its own dye minimum, so your commitment doubles while your design count stays at one. It also explains the hesitation you hear when you ask for 100 pieces: the factory is checking whether fabric and dye minimums leave anything resembling margin</p>

        <p>For men's cut-and-sew tees, expect <strong>300–500 pieces per color</strong>, lower on stock fabrics held at the mill, higher on custom-developed constructions. Pin the exact figure for your fabric and colorway before you lock the design</p>

        <p>Sampling runs <strong>7–15 days</strong>, with fees commonly quoted at <strong>$50–150 per style</strong> for a basic tee. Two questions decide whether that fee is fair: does it convert to a credit against bulk, and how many revision rounds are included? A factory confident in its pattern-making offers the credit. One that refuses is telling you something about its sample-to-bulk consistency</p>

        <p>A complete quote itemizes every line below. If one is missing, you are comparing incomplete numbers:</p>

        <ul class="ar-list"><li>Fabric cost per garment, with GSM and composition stated</li><li>Cut, sew, and finishing labor</li><li>Decoration cost by method and placement</li><li>Labeling and packaging: woven label, neck label, care label, hang tag, poly bag, carton</li><li>Sample fee, and whether it is deductible</li><li>MOQ per color and per size</li><li>Production lead time counted from sample approval</li><li>Payment terms and deposit percentage</li><li>Incoterm (FOB, EXW, or CIF) and port of loading</li></ul>

        <p>A quote without an Incoterm is not a quote. It is a number you cannot compare to anything</p>

        <p>Ready to test a factory on a real spec? Get a Sample</p>

        <h2 id="choosing-by-scenario">Choosing by Scenario: Small Batch, Scaling Brand, Corporate &amp; Cross-Border</h2>

        <p>Your order profile should set your selection weights. The factory that handles a 200-piece test run beautifully is frequently the wrong partner for a 20,000-piece seasonal program</p>

        <h3>Small batch and first-time brand launches</h3>

        <p>Prioritize low MOQ, fast sampling, and willingness to work from reference photos. At this stage you are buying information as much as product: your size curve, sell-through rate, and real cost of goods. Accept the higher unit cost. Factories offering <a href="/t-shirt-manufacturer/">small batch custom t-shirt production</a> that can scale the same style to 5,000 pieces save you a re-sourcing cycle later</p>

        <h3>Scaling brands with repeat programs</h3>

        <p>Reorder consistency, capacity headroom, and planning discipline matter most. The killer is a factory that nails order one and drifts on order three: a different dye lot, a body length 1.5cm off, a rib that feels tighter. Demand a documented sample retention process and an archived lab dip. Bulk typically runs <strong>30–45 days</strong> after sample approval, with <strong>25–35 days</strong> of sea freight on top</p>

        <h3>Corporate, team, and event uniforms</h3>

        <p>Fabric innovation barely matters here; deadline reliability and decoration throughput do. Embroidery and screen print on stock fabrics, with a hard in-hand date and a written contingency plan. Ask whether the factory holds blank inventory or cuts to order, since that single answer moves your lead time by two to three weeks</p>

        <h3>Cross-border e-commerce and private label</h3>

        <p>Packaging, labeling compliance, and per-unit landed cost dominate. You need barcode-ready poly bags, care labels written for the destination market, and carton marks that survive a fulfillment center's receiving dock. That is where private label labeling and packaging pays for itself: every label, tag, sticker, and carton mark specified per project rather than improvised</p>

        <figure class="ar-figure">
