          <figcaption class="ar-cap">Finished T-shirt references prepared for review, from the supplied production environment</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>Whichever profile fits, Request a Quote with quantity, fabric target, and delivery market, and you get a breakdown you can line up against competing offers</p>

        <h2 id="qc-compliance">Quality Control, Compliance &amp; Reorder Consistency</h2>

        <p>Four separate disciplines, routinely confused with each other</p>

        <p><strong>Factory audit.</strong> Before bulk, verify the facility exists and operates as described. A basic audit covers the production floor, machinery inventory, worker count, and whether the processes you are paying for actually sit under that roof. A supplier who refuses a live video walkthrough or a third-party audit has answered your question</p>

        <p>Final random inspection runs against a stated AQL level, commonly 2.5 for major and 4.0 for minor defects on apparel. Your inspector measures against the approved size spec, then checks workmanship, decoration placement, labeling, and packing. Schedule inspection before the balance payment, because after payment your leverage is gone</p>

        <p>Compliance requirements follow the destination market. Typical asks include fabric composition reports, color fastness results, shrinkage data, and restricted substances testing. Clarify which reports the factory supplies directly and which require an outside lab at your cost, and do it before production starts. Retroactive testing has delayed more shipments than any sewing problem</p>

        <figure class="ar-figure">
          <img src="/uploads/t-shirt-manufacturer/quality-finishing-ironing.webp" width="1600" height="1199" alt="Quality control inspection of custom t-shirts at the factory" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption class="ar-cap">Finishing and final check before packing, from the supplied production environment</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>Reorder consistency is the metric that decides long-term partnerships. Ask three specific questions: how are approved samples stored, how long are they retained, and are lab dips archived by dye lot? A factory holding a sealed approval sample and a reference lab dip can rebuild your order 14 months later. A factory running on memory cannot, and you will see it in the third shipment</p>

        <h2 id="common-mistakes">Common Mistakes Buyers Make When Sourcing Custom T-Shirts</h2>

        <p><strong>Comparing unit price without comparing scope.</strong> A $3.20 quote and a $4.10 quote are not comparable when one excludes labeling, packaging, and inland freight. Normalize to the same Incoterm and bill of materials first</p>

        <p><strong>Skipping the pre-production sample.</strong> Verbal and reference-photo approvals produce disputes. Approve a physical garment, sign the tag, and leave one sealed copy with the factory</p>

        <p>A sample also proves less than buyers assume. One garment sewn by a senior pattern maker shows the pattern works; it says nothing about 3,000 pieces off a line of 40 operators. Ask whether sampling and bulk run on the same line</p>

        <p>Lead time gets underestimated more than any other variable. Production plus freight plus clearance routinely totals <strong>8–12 weeks</strong> door to door, and buyers who planned for 30 days end up paying air freight that wipes out their negotiated savings</p>

        <p>Capacity conflicts cause the rest of the slippage. If your order lands in the factory's peak season, your date moves. Ask what else occupies the line during your production window, and whose order gets priority</p>

        <p><strong>Treating slow replies as a minor annoyance.</strong> A supplier taking four days to answer a pre-sales question will take longer once your deposit has cleared. Response time is the cheapest leading indicator available</p>

        <h2 id="faq">FAQ: Custom T-Shirt Manufacturer Questions Buyers Ask Most</h2>

          <details class="ar-faq-item" open><summary><h3>What is the MOQ for custom t-shirts?</h3></summary><p>For men's cut-and-sew T-shirts, MOQ commonly falls in the 300–500 pieces per color range, driven by mill fabric and dye-lot minimums rather than factory policy. Stock fabrics allow lower thresholds; custom-developed fabrics push MOQ higher. Confirm MOQ per color and per size before finalizing your design, because a second colorway can double your commitment</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How long does sampling take, and is the sample fee refundable?</h3></summary><p>Sampling typically runs 7–15 days depending on fabric availability and decoration complexity, with fees commonly quoted between $50 and $150 per basic style. Whether that fee is credited against bulk varies by factory and order size. Ask before you pay, and get the answer in writing along with the number of included revision rounds</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What file format should I provide for my design?</h3></summary><p>Vector files are the standard for print production: AI, EPS, or PDF with outlined fonts. For embroidery, supply a 300 DPI minimum PNG or JPG and expect the factory to digitize it into a stitch file. A competent factory flags low-resolution artwork for cleanup. Include Pantone numbers for every color</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Can you match a specific Pantone color across different fabrics?</h3></summary><p>Exact matching across different fabric compositions is not realistic, because cotton, CVC, and polyester absorb dye differently. A capable factory produces a lab dip on your actual production fabric and secures written approval before bulk dyeing. Expect a result inside a stated tolerance rather than a perfect match, and write that tolerance into your spec sheet</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What's the difference between OEM and ODM for t-shirt manufacturing?</h3></summary><p>OEM means you supply the design, tech pack, and specifications, and the factory manufactures to them. ODM means the factory offers existing patterns that you customize with branding and minor modifications. OEM gives full differentiation but needs a complete tech pack and usually a higher MOQ. ODM starts faster and cheaper, though competitors can source the same garment</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How do you ensure reorder consistency months later?</h3></summary><p>Consistency depends on documentation, not memory. Ask whether the factory retains a sealed approval sample, archives lab dips by dye lot, and stores print screens or embroidery files. Confirm how long those records are kept. A factory that can rebuild your fabric, color, and measurements from an archived reference is a long-term partner</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Do you provide fabric test reports and compliance certificates?</h3></summary><p>Capable manufacturers supply fabric composition reports and internal test data, while third-party testing for restricted substances or market-specific compliance is usually arranged at the buyer's cost. Ask before production rather than before shipment, since retroactive testing can delay delivery by weeks. Request the document list your destination market requires</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What are typical lead times for bulk orders?</h3></summary><p>Bulk production commonly runs 30–45 days after sample approval, followed by 25–35 days of sea freight to North America or Europe, plus customs clearance. Door-to-door totals of 8–12 weeks are normal for ocean shipments. Peak season, complex decoration, and custom fabric development extend these windows. Plan backward from your delivery date</p></details>

